le to manage symptoms with medication. Denies any severe exacerbations or hospitalizations.\n- **Allergic:** Denies any recent severe allergic reactions.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** Reports occasional hives after exposure to peanuts.\n- **General:** Denies any other significant medical issues.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n- **Vital Signs:**\n  - Blood Pressure: 118/78 mmHg\n  - Heart Rate: 76 bpm\n  - Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n  - Temperature: 98.6°F (37°C)\n  - Height: 5\'6"\n  - Weight: 120 lbs\n- **Physical Examination:**\n  - **Respiratory:** No wheezing, no rales, no rhonchi.\n  - **Cardiovascular:** No murmurs, no gallops.\n  - **Allergic:** No hives, no urticaria.\n- **Lung Function Test (LFT):**\n  - Spirometry: FEV1 = 2.51 L (80% of predicted), FEV1/FVC = 0.78 (80% of predicted)\n- **Peak Flow Monitoring:** 21 L/min\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- **Diagnosis:**\n  - Asthma, mild intermittent\n  - Allergic Rhinitis, seasonal\n  - Food Allergy, peanut\n- **Impression:**\n  - The patient is a 20-year-old female with a history of asthma, allergic rhinitis, and food allergy. Her symptoms are well-controlled on Wixela, but she reports occasional wheezing with exertion. She has been able to manage her symptoms with her current regimen.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Medical Treatment:**\n  - Continue Wixela 250/50, 1 puff twice daily as needed.\n  - Alvesco 80 mcg, 2 puffs twice daily as needed.\n  - Zyrtec 10 mg as needed for seasonal allergy symptoms.\n- **Follow-Up:**\n  - Follow-up in 4 months to review asthma control and perform spirometry.\n  - Continue monitoring for signs of asthma exacerbation.\n\n**5. Patient Agreement:**\n- The patient understands the diagnosis and treatment plan and agrees to the recommended therapy.\n\n**6. Referrals:**\n- No referrals at this time.\n\n**7. Results:**\n- No lab results to report at this time.\n\n**8. Medical Decision-Making:**\n- The decision to continue the current regimen and step down to Alvesco was made based on the patient\'s reli