pertension and palpitations. He has been experiencing palpitations since childhood and has been treated with medication. The palpitations were severe enough to require an emergency room visit in March 2021. He reports that he was drinking a few cups of coffee and had not eaten breakfast on that day, which may have contributed to the palpitations. He has been experiencing no palpitations since then.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Cardiovascular: Reports palpitations in the past, currently none.\n- Respiratory: Denies any respiratory symptoms.\n- Gastrointestinal: Reports no gastrointestinal symptoms.\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports no musculoskeletal symptoms.\n- General: Reports no general symptoms.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n- **Vitals:** Blood pressure: 146/85 mmHg, Heart rate: 78 bpm, Respiratory rate: 18 breaths per minute, Temperature: 98.6°F, Height: 5\'9", Weight: 185 lbs.\n- **Constitutional:** Denies any fever or chills.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Denies any chest pain or palpitations.\n- **Respiratory:** Denies any shortness of breath.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** Denies any abdominal pain or nausea.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Denies any joint pain or muscle weakness.\n- **Neurological:** Denies any headaches or dizziness.\n- **Physical Exam:** General appearance: Well-nourished, well-developed male. Cardiovascular: No murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Respiratory: No wheezes or rales. Gastrointestinal: No tenderness or guarding. Musculoskeletal: No joint tenderness or swelling. Neurological: No focal deficits.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- **Diagnosis:** Resistant Hypertension\n- **Plan:**\n  - **Medication:** Continue on Benicar HCT 40/25 mg orally once daily.\n  - **Medication:** Add Spironolactone 25 mg orally once daily.\n  - **Patient Education:** Discussed the importance of adhering to the medication regimen and lifestyle modifications to control blood pressure.\n  - **Follow-up:** Schedule follow-up in 2 weeks to monitor blood pressure and electrolyte levels.\n  - **Discussion:** Discussed the potential risks and benefits of Spironolactone, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. Discussed the importance of adhering to the medication regimen and lifestyle modifications to control blood pressure.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Patient Agreements:**\n  - Agreed to adhere to the medication regimen.\n  - Agreed to follow up in 2 weeks to monitor blood pressure and electrolyte levels.\n- **Patient Referrals:**\n  - Referral to the Resistant Hypertension Clinic if the patient does not respond to the added medication.\n- **Patient Education:**\n  - Discussed the importance of adhering to the medication regimen and lifestyle modifications to control blood pressure.\n  - Discussed the potential risks and benefits of Spironolactone.\n  - Discussed the importance of adhering to the medication regimen and lifestyle modifications to control blood pressure.\n- **Patient Counseling:**\n  - Discussed the importance of adhering to the medication regimen and lifestyle modifications to control blood pressure.\n  - Discussed the potential risks and benefits of Spironolactone.\n  - Discussed the importance of adhering to the medication regimen and lifestyle modifications to control blood pressure.\n\n**5. References:**\n- American Heart Association (AHA) website: heart.org.\n- The DASH Diet: A diet that has been shown to lower blood pressure. Discussed the importance of adhering to this diet.\n- Spironolactone: Discussed the potential risks and benefits of this medication, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances.\n\n**6. Treatment Plan:**\n- **Medication:** Continue on Benicar HCT 40/25 mg orally once daily.\n- **Medication:** Add Spironolactone 25 mg orally once daily.\n- **Patient Education:** Discussed the importance of adhering to the medication regimen and lifestyle modifications to control blood pressure.\n- **Follow-up:** Schedule follow-up in 2 weeks to monitor blood pressure and electrolyte levels.\n\n**7. Referrals:**\n- Referral to the Resistant Hypertension Clinic if the patient does not respond to the added medication.\n\n**8. Disposition:**\n- The patient will continue on Benicar HCT 40/25 mg orally once daily and Spironolactone 25 mg orally once daily.\n- The patient will follow up in 2 weeks to monitor blood pressure and electrolyte levels.\n- The patient was referred to the Resistant Hypertension Clinic if the patient does not respond to the added medication.\n\n**9. Conclusion:**\n- The patient has resistant hypertension and is on three medications at maximum dose. He was started on Spironolactone 25 mg orally once daily. He will follow up in 2 weeks to monitor blood pressure and electrolyte levels. If the patient does not respond to Spironolactone, he will be referred to the Resistant Hypertension Clinic.\n\n**10. Signature:**\n- The patient\'s signature indicates that he understands and agrees with the treatment plan.\n\n**11. Signature:**\n- The doctor\'s signature indicates that the treatment plan has been reviewed and agreed upon.\n\n**12. Date:**\n- The date of the doctor\'s signature indicates the date the treatment plan was agreed upon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ HPC: 49 year old male with resistant hypertension, palpitations, and a history of hypertension since young age. He has been taking Benicar HCT and amlodipine, but his blood pressure has not been controlled. He has been drinking less salt and trying to walk regularly. He has a family history of hypertension. He has been taking potassium 4.0. He has been having blood pressure around 146-155 in the morning. His blood pressure has not been controlled on three medications.\n\nImpression: Resistant hypertension.\n\nPlan: Add spironolactone to his regimen. He will need to have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in two weeks. He will need to monitor his potassium and sodium levels. He will need to hydrate regularly and report any dizziness. He will need to report any breast tenderness or swelling. He will need to monitor his blood pressure regularly. He will need to have a follow up in two weeks to check his blood pressure and electrolytes. If his blood pressure is not controlled, he will need to go to the resistant hypertension clinic. He will need to look at the dash eating plan and try to lower his salt intake. He will need to do exercise regularly. He will need to monitor his blood pressure regularly. He will need to have a follow up in two weeks to check his blood pressure and electrolytes. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood pressure and palpitations.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJordan Roberts is a 49-year-old with a long history of hypertension and an acute episode of palpitations.\n\nIn March, Mr. Roberts went to the emergency room for palpitations and hypertension. He attributed this to a panic attack, but also stated he consumed several cups of coffee on an empty stomach the morning of. He denies additional episodes of palpitations.\n\nHe has been monitoring his blood pressure at home daily, obtaining systolic blood pressure readings of 146 to 155. He does report a family history of hypertension. Additionally, he has been monitoring his salt intake and feels he is doing well at reducing it. He also walks on his treadmill as often as he can.\n\nHe has a new primary care physician who started him on a new medication Benicar HCT, which he has been using for the past month.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypertension.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nSeveral family members positive for hypertension.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nBenicar HCT 40 mg/25 mg\nAmlodipine 10 mg daily.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nCardiovascular\nNormal rate, regular rhythm, S2 normal, normal heart sounds, and normal pulses.\n\nPulmonary/Chest\nBreath sounds normal. \n\nAbdominal\nThere is no tenderness.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nThere is no edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThyroid profile 03/01/2021.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Resistant hypertension.\n\nMr. Roberts presents with resistant hypertension, with systolic blood pressure readings between 146-155. He is currently on maximal doses for 3 medications.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have added spironolactone to his regimen which will require monitoring of his potassium and sodium. We will schedule him for a follow up in 2 weeks to complete a basic metabolic panel and check his blood pressure. Next medication to consider adding is carvedilol. I have tried to avoid this, given the potential for issues with erectile dysfunction. I counseled the patient about spironolactone side effects, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances, as well as his increased risk of dehydration, with one sign to watch for being dizziness. If we are not able to improve his blood pressure with spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to the resistant hypertension clinic.\n\nI provided and reviewed with Mr. Roberts the American Heart association website (heart.org) and encouraged him to access for tips on lowering his salt intake and increasing his potassium intake. His maximum salt intake should be 1500 to <2000 mg per day. He was provided materials to follow the DASH diet and encouraged to meet 30-min of walking most days.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nSchedule follow up appointment in 2 weeks for blood pressure check and basic metabolic panel. Reduce sodium intake to 1500 to <2000 mg per day. Follow DASH diet.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n 