:**\nThe patient presents with a follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nMr. Christopher Watson, an 86-year-old male, returns for follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. He was initially seen in May 2020 for progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. He underwent shunt surgery on August 1, 2020, with a Medtronic Strata Programmable Shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration, set at a level of 2.0.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Constitutional: Denies fever, unintentional weight loss.\n- General: Denies genital sores, decreased or painful urination.\n- Neurological: Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.\n- Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vitals:**\n- Blood Pressure: 124/80 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 64 bpm\n- Respirations: 18 breaths/min\n- Weight: 174 pounds\n- Pain: 0/10\n\n**Neurological:**\n- The patient is pleasant and cooperative, able to converse easily, with his daughter adding some details.\n- Shunt site: Clean, dry, and intact with a confirmed setting of 2.0.\n- Memory and cognitive testing: MMSE score of 26/30 (slightly improved from previous score of 23/30).\n- Attention span and concentration: Able to get 2 out of 3 memory words with cuing and 1 with multiple choice.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n- Adult Hydrocephalus\n- Gait Impairment\n- Urinary Incontinence and Urgency\n- Cognitive Impairment\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- Follow-up in 3 months for further assessment and possible adjustment of shunt setting if needed.\n- The patient is advised to continue with his current treatment plan and follow-up as recommended.\n- The patient is informed that it is unlikely he will be able to drive at this time due to his cognitive and motor impairments, but he is encouraged to participate in a driver safety program if he wishes.\n- The patient is also advised to enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation if he wishes.\n- He is to continue with his current treatment plan and follow-up as recommended.\n\n**5. Medical Decision Making:**\nThe patient\'s overall condition has shown some improvement since the shunt surgery, and he is currently stable. The slight blood fluid collection in the right parietal region on the recent CT scan does not appear to be causing any symptoms and is not considered a significant issue at this time.\n\n**6. Patient Education:**\n- The patient was informed of his condition and the reasons for his symptoms.\n- He was advised of the need for ongoing follow-up and the possibility of future adjustments to his treatment plan.\n- He was informed of the limitations on his ability to drive and the availability of driver safety programs.\n- He was also informed of the importance of adherence to his current treatment plan and follow-up recommendations.\n\n**7. Referrals:**\n- No referrals are necessary at this time.\n\n**8. Tests Ordered:**\n- None ordered at this time.\n\n**9. Medications:**\n- None prescribed at this time.\n\n**10. Follow-up:**\n- Follow-up in 3 months for further assessment and possible adjustment of shunt setting if needed.\n\n**11. Other:**\n- The patient was able to walk unassisted to the dining room, but still requires assistance with daily activities.\n- The patient is able to converse easily and has improved cognitive function, but still has some difficulties with memory and attention.\n- The patient is able to maintain bladder control, but requires use of a diaper.\n- There is no evidence of pain or discomfort related to the shunt site.\n- The patient is advised to continue with his current treatment plan and follow-up as recommended.\n\n**12. Disposition:**\n- The patient will continue to be monitored for any changes in his condition and will be reassessed in 3 months.\n- The patient is advised to adhere to his current treatment plan and follow-up recommendations.\n- The patient is informed of the limitations on hi