n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\n- The patient, Scott, presents with a 3-day history of right knee pain and swelling after an injury during a soccer game.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\n- The patient reports that he was playing soccer over the weekend and attempted to kick the ball, but felt a sudden pop in his right knee. He describes a moderate pain level of 6-7/10, which has been relieved somewhat by ibuprofen. The pain has been accompanied by swelling, which has decreased slightly over the past few days. He reports difficulty with knee movement, stating that he can bend but it hurts at the end of the range of motion and can straighten it but it doesn\'t feel normal. He also reports that he can bear weight on it but it feels loose.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- General: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports pain and swelling in the right knee, limited range of motion, and decreased strength.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General Appearance:** The patient appears in no acute distress, alert and cooperative.\n- **Vital Signs:**\n  - Blood Pressure: 120/80 mmHg\n  - Temperature: 98.6°F\n  - Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n  - Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n  - SpO2: 98% on room air\n- **Constitutional:**\n  - Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\n- **Musculoskeletal:**\n  - **Right Knee:**\n    - **Inspection:** Significant swelling and ecchymosis noted.\n    - **Palpation:** Pain with palpation at the medial joint line.\n    - **Range of Motion:** Limited active range of motion, particularly in flexion and extension.\n    - **Strength:** Muscle strength 5/5 in both lower extremities.\n    - **Neurovascular:** Bilateral lower extremities neurologically intact.\n- **Gait:** Noted a marked limp on the right leg.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n**Diagnosis:**\n- Anterior Cruciate Ligament Tear (ACL Tear)\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Medical Treatment:**\n  - Order MRI to evaluate the integrity 