ective:**\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\n- The patient, Scott, presents with a 3-day history of right knee pain and swelling after an injury during a soccer game.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\n- The patient reports that he was playing soccer over the weekend and attempted to kick the ball, but felt a sudden pop in his right knee. He describes a moderate pain level of 6-7/10, which has been relieved somewhat by ibuprofen. The pain has been accompanied by swelling, which has decreased slightly over the past few days. He reports difficulty with knee movement, stating that he can bend but it hurts at the end of the range of motion and can straighten it but it doesn\'t feel normal. He also reports that he can bear weight on it but it feels loose.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- General: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports pain and swelling in the right knee, limited range of motion, and decreased strength.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General Appearance:** The patient appears in no acute distress, alert and cooperative.\n- **Vital Signs:**\n  - Blood Pressure: 120/80 mmHg\n  - Temperature: 98.6°F\n  - Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n  - Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n  - SpO2: 98% on room air\n- **Constitutional:**\n  - Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\n- **Musculoskeletal:**\n  - **Right Knee:**\n    - **Inspection:** Significant swelling and ecchymosis noted.\n    - **Palpation:** Pain with palpation at the medial joint line.\n    - **Range of Motion:** Limited active range of motion, particularly in flexion and extension.\n    - **Strength:** Muscle strength 5/5 in both lower extremities.\n    - **Neurovascular:** Bilateral lower extremities neurologically intact.\n- **Gait:** Noted a marked limp on the right leg.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n**Diagnosis:**\n- Anterior Cruciate Ligament Tear (ACL Tear)\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Medical Treatment:**\n  - Order MRI to evaluate the integrity of the ACL.\n  - Prescribe Meloxicam 50 mg daily for 3 months to reduce pain and swelling.\n  - Advise the use of ice packs as needed for pain relief.\n- **Activity Modification:**\n  - Advise the patient to moderate his activities to avoid further strain on the knee.\n  - Recommend use of a knee immobilizer to reduce pain and swelling.\n  - Prescribe crutches to assist with ambulation.\n- **Follow-up:**\n  - Schedule a follow-up appointment in 2 weeks to review MRI results and determine the need for surgical intervention.\n- **Patient Education:**\n  - Discussed the importance of adhering to the recommended treatment plan and the potential need for surgery.\n  - Educated the patient on the risks and benefits of surgery, including the potential for a prolonged recovery period and the need for physical therapy.\n\n**5. Patient Agreement:**\n- The patient understands the recommended treatment plan and is willing to adhere to it.\n\n**6. Referrals:**\n- Referral for MRI to evaluate the integrity of the ACL.\n- Referral to Orthopedic Surgery for further evaluation and potential surgical intervention.\n\n**7. Medications:**\n- Meloxicam 50 mg oral tablet, 1 tablet daily, as prescribed.\n\n**8. Instructions:**\n- Moderate activities to avoid further strain on the knee.\n- Use a knee immobilizer as recommended.\n- Use crutches for ambulation.\n- Apply ice packs as needed for pain relief.\n- Follow-up in 2 weeks for MRI results and potential surgical evaluation. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ HPC: 9/10\nHPI: \n- 3 days ago, while playing soccer, felt a pop in the right knee.\n- Swelling has decreased since then, but still swollen.\n- Can\'t move the knee as well as usual.\n- Pain on a scale of 6-7/10.\n- No numbness or tingling in the legs.\n- No fever, chills, or cough.\n- No recent trauma to the knee.\n- No previous knee problems.\n- No recent illness.\n- No recent surgery.\n- No previous knee surgery.\n- No previous knee problems.\n- No previous trauma to the knee.\n- No previous swelling in the