 sport i i just do you guy do you do a lot of hockey down there in town here\n[patient] used to when i lived in detroit\n[doctor] oh\n[patient] hockey town but not so much anymore\n[doctor] no yeah detroit is a hockey town yeah the red wings okay well i\'m gon na go ahead and i\'ll send my nurse in and go ahead and get you discharged and like i said i\'d like to see you back here in seven to ten days after that first physical therapy appointment i\'ve got a great phenomenal office staff you just call in once that gets scheduled they will get you in to that next available appointment so take care and i will talk to you later\n[patient] thanks\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\n- Dennis presents with a 2-month history of pain in the back of his right heel that persists after training for a triathlon.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\n- The patient reports that the pain is constant and occurs after running and cycling, and is exacerbated by walking or standing for long periods. The pain is located in the Achilles tendon and radiates up the calf.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Reports pain in the lower extremities, specifically in the Achilles tendon.\n- Reports no fever, chills, or weight loss.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n**Physical Examination:**\n- The patient appears in no acute distress.\n- The right Achilles tendon shows tenderness to palpation.\n- The patient reports pain with passive ankle dorsiflexion.\n- The patient is able to perform a single heel-to-toe walk without difficulty.\n- No edema or erythema noted.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- **Diagnosis:** Achilles tendinitis.\n- **Chief Complaint (CC):** Pain in the back of the right heel.\n- **History of Present Illness (HPI):** The patient reports a 2-month history of pain in the back of his right heel, which persists after training for a triathlon.\n- **Review of Systems (ROS):** Reports pain in the lower extremities, specifically in the Achilles tendon. No fever, chills, or weight loss.\n- **Physical Examination:** The patient appears in no acute distress. The right Achilles tendon shows tenderness to palpation, and the patient reports pain with passive ankle dorsiflexion.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Medication:** Ibuprofen 200 mg, 3 tablets orally every 6 hours as needed for pain and inflammation.\n- **Referrals:** Referral to physical therapy for 2 sessions to address strength and stretching exercises.\n- **Follow-up:** Follow-up in 7-10 days to review progress and reassess.\n- **Education:** The patient was educated on the importance of proper stretching and strengthening exercises to alleviate pain and inflammation. Discussed the need to stop training for a week or two to allow the Achilles tendon to heal.\n- **Instructions:** The patient was advised to take Ibuprofen as prescribed and to follow up in 7-10 days. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ HPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\nHPC: 5/10\n