 grade 3 systolic ejection murmur on cardiac exam, which is unchanged from previous exams.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- Vital Signs: Blood Pressure: 128/82 mmHg, Heart Rate: 72 bpm, Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min, Temperature: 98.6°F, Height: 5\'8", Weight: 150 lbs.\n- General: Alert, oriented, and cooperative.\n- Cardiovascular: Grade 3 systolic ejection murmur, otherwise normal.\n- Respiratory: Mild end-expiratory wheezes, otherwise normal.\n- Abdominal: Mild tenderness in the epigastrium, otherwise soft, no tenderness in the right lower quadrant or right upper quadrant.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Chief Diagnosis:** Acute Gastritis.\n- **Diagnosis:** Barbara presents with acute gastritis, likely related to her excessive caffeine intake and possible spicy food consumption.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Medical Treatment:**\n  - Prescribed Zantac 150mg twice daily for 14 days.\n  - Discussed the importance of reducing caffeine intake, recommending she limit herself to less than two or three drinks per week.\n  - Recommended reducing alcohol consumption to less than two or three drinks per week.\n  - Discussed the importance of reducing spicy food intake.\n\n- **Labs:**\n  - Ordered Urinalysis.\n  - Ordered Urine Pregnancy Test.\n  - Ordered Complete Blood Count (CBC).\n  - Ordered Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP).\n\n- **Follow-Up:**\n  - Scheduled follow-up in 4 weeks.\n\n- **Patient Education and Counseling:**\n  - Educated Barbara on the importance of reducing caffeine and alcohol intake and reducing spicy food consumption to manage her gastritis.\n  - Discussed the importance of adhering to her metformin regimen, specifically increasing the morning dose to 1000mg and the evening dose to 500mg.\n\n- **Patient Agreement:**\n  - Barbara understood and agreed with the plan, acknowledging the importance of lifestyle changes to manage her gastritis. She expressed willingness to reduce her caffeine and alcohol intake and spicy food consumption.\n\n- **Patient Referral:**\n  - None necessary at this time.\n\n**5. Instructions:**\n\n- To reduce symptoms of gastritis, Barbara is advised to:\n  - Limit caffeine intake to less than two or three drinks per week.\n  - Reduce alcohol consumption to less than two or three drinks per week.\n  - Avoid spicy food or reduce consumption.\n- To adhere to her metformin regimen, Barbara is advised to:\n  - Take metformin 1000mg in the morning and 500mg in the evening.\n  - Continue to monitor her blood sugars and report any changes to her provider.\n\n**Patient Compliance:**\n- Barbara understands the importance of adhering to her medical treatment plan and has agreed to follow the recommended lifestyle changes.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\n- Barbara understands and agrees with the plan, acknowledging the importance of lifestyle changes to manage her gastritis.\n\n**Next Steps:**\n- Follow-up in 4 weeks to assess the effectiveness of the treatment plan and monitor her blood sugars.\n\n**Additional Medical Information:**\n- The patient\'s medical history includes a history of Type 2 Diabetes and Asthma, which is currently well-controlled.\n\n**Additional Relevant Medical History:**\n- The patient has a history of moderate to severe abdominal pain, which is exacerbated by exercise and possibly related to her caffeine intake.\n\n**Medical Treatment:**\n- Prescribed Zantac 150mg twice daily for 14 days.\n\n**Medical Treatment Options:**\n- Discussed the importance of reducing caffeine intake, recommending she limit herself to less than two or three drinks per week.\n- Recommended reducing alcohol consumption to less than two or three drinks per week.\n- Discussed the importance of reducing spicy food intake.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- Educated Barbara on the importance of reducing caffeine and alcohol intake and reducing spicy food consumption to manage her gastritis.\n- Discussed the importance of adhering to her metformin regimen, specifically increasing the morning dose to 1000mg