e you on the flovent that seems to be doing pretty well continue to take that daily and then also albuterol as needed so how does that sound for a plan any other questions comments suggestions\n[patient] that sounds good and i also feel like i\'m eating been eating a lot of spicy food\n[doctor] hmmm\n[patient] lately that could be contributing towards my gastritis so i\'m gon na cut back on that as well\n[doctor] okay yeah i\'d also recommend while this is hurting you definitely cut back on the spices the spicy food easier said than done sometimes i know but yep yep alright sounds good well listen i\'ll see you back in four weeks give me a holler if if you have any questions or anything else in the meantime\n[patient] i will\n[doctor] alright you take care sure you got\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\n- Barbara, a 31-year-old female, presents with a chief complaint of abdominal pain for the past week.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\n- Barbara has a history of Type 2 Diabetes and Asthma. She reports having moderate to severe abdominal pain that has been gradually increasing in severity over the past week. The pain is located on both sides of her upper abdomen and worsens with exercise, particularly with jogging or running. She has had nausea and vomiting on and off for the past week, with about three episodes of nausea and a couple of episodes of vomiting.\n\n- Barbara reports that her coffee intake has increased significantly over the past week, drinking multiple venti-sized cups daily. She believes this may be related to her abdominal pain.\n\n- She also reports that she has been having a lot of spicy food lately, which may be contributing to her gastritis.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Gastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\n- Endocrine: Reports history of Type 2 Diabetes, controlled with metformin 500mg twice daily.\n- Cardiovascular: Reports a history of hypertension, with a grade 3 systolic ejection murmur on cardiac exam, which is unchanged from previous exams.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- Vital Signs: Blood Pressure: 128/82 mmHg, Heart Rate: 72 bpm, Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min, Temperature: 98.6°F, Height: 5\'8", Weight: 150 lbs.\n- General: Alert, oriented, and cooperative.\n- Cardiovascular: Grade 3 systolic ejection murmur, otherwise normal.\n- Respiratory: Mild end-expiratory wheezes, otherwise normal.\n- Abdominal: Mild tenderness in the epigastrium, otherwise soft, no tenderness in the right lower quadrant or right upper quadrant.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Chief Diagnosis:** Acute Gastritis.\n- **Diagnosis:** Barbara presents with acute gastritis, likely related to her excessive caffeine intake and possible spicy food consumption.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Medical Treatment:**\n  - Prescribed Zantac 150mg twice daily for 14 days.\n  - Discussed the importance of reducing caffeine intake, recommending she limit herself to less than two or three drinks per week.\n  - Recommended reducing alcohol consumption to less than two or three drinks per week.\n  - Discussed the importance of reducing spicy food intake.\n\n- **Labs:**\n  - Ordered Urinalysis.\n  - Ordered Urine Pregnancy Test.\n  - Ordered Complete Blood Count (CBC).\n  - Ordered Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP).\n\n- **Follow-Up:**\n  - Scheduled follow-up in 4 weeks.\n\n- **Patient Education and Counseling:**\n  - Educated Barbara on the importance of reducing caffeine and alcohol intake and reducing spicy food consumption to manage her gastritis.\n  - Discussed the importance of adhering to her metformin regimen, specifically increasing the morning dose to 1000mg and the evening dose to 500mg.\n\n- **Patient Agreement:**\n  - Barbara understood and agreed with the plan, acknowledging the importance of lifestyle changes to manage her gastritis. She expressed willingness to reduce her caffeine and alcohol intake and spicy food consumption.\n\n- **Patient Referral:**\n  - None necessary at this time.\n\n**5. Instructions:**\n\n- To reduce symptoms of gastritis, Barbara is advised to:\n  - Limit caffeine intake to less than two or three drinks per week.\n  - Reduce alcohol consumption to less than two or three drinks per week.\n  - Avoid spicy food or reduce consumption.\n- To adhere to her metformin regimen, Barbara is advised to:\n  - Take metformin 1000mg in the morning and 500mg in the evening.\n  - Continue to monitor her blood sugars and report any changes to her provider.\n\n**Patient Compliance:**\n- Barbara understands the importance of adhering to her medical treatment plan and has agreed to follow the recommended lifestyle changes.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\n- Barbara understands and agrees with the plan, acknowledging the importance of lifestyle changes to manage her gastritis.\n\n**Next Steps:**\n- Follow-up in 4 weeks to assess the effectiveness of the treatment plan and monitor her blood sugars.\n\n**Additional Medical Information:**\n- The patient\'s medical history includes a history of Type 2 Diabetes and Asthma, which is currently well-controlled.\n\n**Additional Relevant Medical History:**\n- The patient has a history of moderate to severe abdominal pain, which is exacerbated by exercise and possibly related to her caffeine intake.\n\n**Medical Treatment:**\n- Prescribed Zantac 150mg twice daily for 14 days.\n\n**Medical Treatment Options:**\n- Discussed the importance of reducing caffeine intake, recommending she limit herself to less than two or three drinks per week.\n- Recommended reducing alcohol consumption to less than two or three drinks per week.\n- Discussed the importance of reducing spicy food intake.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- Educated Barbara on the importance of reducing caffeine and alcohol intake and reducing spicy food consumption to manage her gastritis.\n- Discussed the importance of adhering to her metformin regimen, specifically increasing the morning dose to 1000mg and the evening dose to 500mg.\n\n**Patient Referrals:**\n- None necessary at this time.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\n- Barbara understands and agrees with the plan, acknowledging the importance of lifestyle changes to manage her gastritis.\n\n**Patient Compliance:**\n- Barbara understands the importance of adhering to her medical treatment plan and has agreed to follow the recommended lifestyle changes.\n\n**Next Steps:**\n- Follow-up in 4 weeks to assess the effectiveness of the treatment plan and monitor her blood sugars.\n\n**Additional Medical Information:**\n- The patient\'s medical history includes a history of Type 2 Diabetes and Asthma, which is currently well-controlled.\n\n**Additional Relevant Medical History:**\n- The patient has a history of moderate to severe abdominal pain, which is exacerbated by exercise and possibly related to her caffeine intake.\n\n**Medical Treatment:**\n- Prescribed Zantac 150mg twice daily for 14 days.\n\n**Medical Treatment Options:**\n- Discussed the importance of reducing caffeine intake, recommending she limit herself to less than two or three drinks per week.\n- Recommended reducing alcohol consumption to less than two or three drinks per week.\n- Discussed the importance of reducing spicy food intake.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- Educated Barbara on the importance of reducing caffeine and alcohol intake and reducing spicy food consumption to manage her gastritis.\n- Discussed the importance of adhering to her metformin regimen, specifically increasing the morning dose to 1000mg and the evening dose to 500mg.\n\n**Patient Referrals:**\n- None necessary at this time.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\n- Barbara understands and agrees with the plan, acknowledging the importance of lifestyle changes to manage her gastritis.\n\n**Patient Compliance:**\n- Barbara understands the importance of adhering to her medical treatment plan and has agreed to follow the recommended lifestyle changes.\n\n**Next Steps:**\n- Follow-up in 4 weeks to assess the effectiveness of the treatment plan and monitor her blood sugars.\n\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ PC: 31 year old female with a history of type 2 diabetes and asthma, presents with a one week history of worsening abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting. \nHPC: Abdominal pain is increasing in severity, nausea and vomiting present, worsening after drinking coffee, worsening with exercise. \nImp: Acute gastritis, likely related to caffeine and alcohol intake. \nPlan: \n1. Cut back on coffee and alcohol intake.\n2. Start Zantac 150mg twice daily.\n3. Check urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC and comprehensive metabolic panel.\n4. Continue metformin 1000mg in the morning and 500mg in the evening.\n5. Continue flovent 44mcg twice daily, albuterol as needed.\n6. Follow up in four weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes type 2, asthma, and gastritis.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of moderately heavy alcohol use. She also enjoys coffee.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice daily, Flovent twice daily, and albuterol as-needed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. Denies diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Mild end expiratory wheezes, very faint.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Mild tenderness in the epigastrium. Abdomen is soft. No tenderness in the right lower quadrant. No significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Abdominal pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be acute gastritis, likely related to her increased caffeine intake. She has a history of moderately heavy alcohol use as well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed appropriate limitations for alcohol intake as well as spicy foods.\n- Medical Treatment: Reduce caffeine intake. Reduce alcohol intake. Prescription for Zantac twice daily provided. I\'m going to order a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and a comprehensive metabolic panel.\n\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n- Medical Reasoning: She is doing well, but I want to make a slight adjustment to her dosage.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue with home monitoring and bring those readings to her next appointment.\n- Medical Treatment: Metformin 1000 mg in the morning and 500 mg at night.\nContinue home blood sugar monitoring.\n\n3. Asthma.\n- Medical Reasoning: She seems to be doing well on her current regimen and has not had any asthma attacks lately.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: She was advised to take Flovent daily despite no recent asthma attacks as this will decrease the frequency of her attacks.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue on daily Flovent. Continue albuterol as-needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


