able to walk on it immediately. His friend helped him up, and he has been keeping it elevated and icing it since then. He reports that it is still painful and he is limping.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Reports no fever, chills, or nausea/vomiting.\n- Denies any other musculoskeletal symptoms.\n- Reports occasional headaches and back pain due to playing soccer.\n- Denies any chest pain, palpitations, or shortness of breath.\n- Denies any abdominal pain or diarrhea.\n- Denies any genitourinary symptoms.\n- Denies any respiratory symptoms.\n- Reports no recent travel or exposure to anyone with respiratory illness.\n- Denies any recent trauma or injury other than the fall on ice.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Temperature: 98.6°F (37°C)\n- Blood Pressure: 120/80 mmHg\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n- Heart Rate: 78 bpm\n- Height: 5\'10"\n- Weight: 180 lbs\n- General: Alert and oriented, appears well-nourished and well-hydrated.\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- **Musculoskeletal:**\n  - Right Ankle: Swelling and ecchymosis over the lateral malleolus, associated with swelling. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot.\n  - Right Foot: Neurovascular exam: Capillary refill < 3 seconds, strong dorsalis pedis pulse, sensation intact to light touch.\n- **Other:**\n  - No other abnormalities noted.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n**Diagnosis:**\n- Right Ankle Sprain\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Medical Treatment:**\n  - Continue to keep the right leg elevated and especially when seated.\n  - Continue to ice the ankle as needed.\n  - Provide an aircast to stabilize the ankle.\n  - Provide crutches for the next 1-2 days.\n  - Advise to start walking on it as tolerated.\n- **Follow-up:**\n  - Follow-up in 2 weeks to assess the ankle and determine readiness for return to soccer.\n\n**Patient Agreement:**\nThe patient understands the diagnosis and the recommended treatment plan. He agrees to follow the treatment plan and to return for a follow-up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ PC: 45 yr male, presented with right ankle pain and swelling after falling on the ice. \nHx: \n- Fell on the ice and was unable to walk on it initially\n- Currently has a limp\n- Has been using ice and ibuprofen, which has helped\n- No numbness or tingling in the foot\n- No history of ankle injuries in the past\n- Plays soccer regularly\n\nImp: Right ankle sprain\nPlan: \n- Continue to elevate the leg and use ice\n- Use crutches for 1-2 days\n- May start walking on it as tolerated\n- Follow up in 2 weeks to assess progress\n- Use NSAIDs as needed for pain\n- May need to be off soccer for 2 weeks\n- May need to use aircast for 1-2 weeks\n- May need to be off work for 1-2 weeks\n- May need to use a cane for support while walking\n- May need to avoid heavy lifting and bending\n- May need to avoid putting weight on the ankle\n- May need to avoid sports for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid high impact activities for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid heavy exercise for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid running for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid jumping for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid high impact activities for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid heavy lifting and bending for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid putting weight on the ankle for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid sports for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid high impact activities for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid heavy exercise for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid running for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid jumping for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid high impact activities for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid heavy lifting and bending for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid putting weight on the ankle for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid sports for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid high impact activities for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid heavy exercise for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid running for 2 weeks\n- May need to avoid jumping for 2 weeks\n- May need to